╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_entanglement_topology                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ The current implementation uses a linear nearest-neighbor entanglement topology with      │
│                          │ uniform parameter sharing across all qubits. This approach, while parameter-efficient,    │
│                          │ may be suboptimal for non-local correlations in the data.                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I propose a fundamentally different algorithmic approach:                                 │
│                          │ 1. **Non-linear entanglement patterns**: Instead of linear CZ chains, use a mix of        │
│                          │ short-range and long-range entanglement with strategic qubit pairings (forming a more     │
│                          │ connected topology).                                                                      │
│                          │ 2. **Heterogeneous parameter sharing**: Rather than full global sharing, use selective    │
│                          │ sharing that groups qubits by their entanglement roles - some parameters are shared       │
│                          │ within local clusters while others remain independent for key qubits.                     │
│                          │ 3. **Rotation axis diversity**: Mix RX and RY rotations (not just RY) to explore          │
│                          │ different manifolds of the Bloch sphere, reducing the effective expressivity bottleneck.  │
│                          │ 4. **Two-qubit parametrized gates**: Replace fixed CZ gates with parametrized XX/YY/ZZ    │
│                          │ Ising gates on critical entanglement points, allowing the circuit to adaptively learn the │
│                          │ optimal coupling strength.                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This approach maintains 20 parameters while increasing circuit expressivity through:      │
│                          │ - Better connectivity (qubit 0 connects to 4, creating long-range links)                  │
│                          │ - Ising gates for adaptive entanglement strength                                          │
│                          │ - Rotation axis variation to break symmetry                                               │
│                          │ - Clustered parameter sharing that preserves local structure                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Expected improvements:                                                                    │
│                          │ - Faster convergence (fewer steps needed due to better expressivity per parameter)        │
│                          │ - Potentially lower generalization gap through reduced overfitting to linear patterns     │
│                          │ - Better parameter efficiency utilization through targeted expressivity                   │
