│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ unified_shared_params                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The analysis of the three programs reveals a clear pattern:                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Prior Program 1 & 2** (score: 0.68): Use aggressive parameter sharing across all 8   │
│                          │ qubits with 3 distinct parameters (ry_shared, rz_pre, rz_post) or (ry_shared,             │
│                          │ rz_pre_shared, rz_post_shared), resulting in only 20 trainable parameters, excellent      │
│                          │ convergence at step 60, and perfect accuracy.                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Current Program** (score: 0.64): Uses alternating parameter patterns (ry_a/ry_b,     │
│                          │ rz_pre_a/rz_pre_b, rz_post_a/rz_post_b), resulting in 38 parameters, slower convergence   │
│                          │ at step 90, and worse parameter efficiency (0.03 vs 0.05).                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key insight is that **parameter sharing is the dominant factor** for optimization     │
│                          │ quality on this task:                                                                     │
│                          │ - Fewer parameters (20 vs 38) lead to:                                                    │
│                          │   - Better parameter efficiency scores                                                    │
│                          │   - Faster convergence (60 vs 90 steps)                                                   │
│                          │   - Higher overall combined score (0.68 vs 0.64)                                          │
│                          │ - All three maintain perfect accuracy, so the differentiator is parameter economy and     │
│                          │ convergence speed                                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The optimal strategy is to return to full parameter sharing but ensure we use the most    │
│                          │ robust parameterization. Using fully shared parameters across all qubits (ry_shared,      │
│                          │ rz_pre, rz_post) provides the best balance:                                               │
│                          │ - Minimizes parameter count to just 3 parameters                                          │
│                          │ - Maximizes parameter efficiency                                                          │
│                          │ - Achieves fastest convergence                                                            │
│                          │ - Maintains perfect generalization                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This is a crossover that selects the superior approach from the prior programs over the   │
│                          │ current less-efficient implementation.                                                    │
