╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ separate_rz_parameters_for_convergence                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The current approach ties pre- and post-entanglement RZ rotations to a single shared      │
│                          │ parameter ("rz_shared"), achieving excellent parameter efficiency (14 params, score 0.72) │
│                          │ but with slower convergence (90 steps). By splitting the RZ layer into two separate but   │
│                          │ still fully-shared parameters—"rz_pre" before entanglement and "rz_post" after—we         │
│                          │ increase from 2 trainable parameters (ry_shared, rz_shared) to 3 (ry_shared, rz_pre,      │
│                          │ rz_post). This modest increase should:                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Improve the optimization landscape by allowing more flexible rotation gates            │
│                          │ 2. Reduce convergence time from 90 steps closer to the ~60 steps observed with prior      │
│                          │ successful configurations                                                                 │
│                          │ 3. Maintain excellent parameter efficiency (3 parameters vs. 56 in the first program and  │
│                          │ only 7 more than the minimum)                                                             │
│                          │ 4. Preserve the perfect accuracy and zero generalization gap                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This aligns with the trend seen in prior programs where decoupling overly-constrained     │
│                          │ parameters improved convergence while maintaining accuracy.                               │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0152                                                                                   │
