│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ hybrid_alternating_ry_rz_minimal                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ Analysis of the three programs shows:                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Current best (score 0.72)**: Uses 14 parameters with full sharing (ry_shared,        │
│                          │ rz_shared). Achieves perfect accuracy with minimal parameters but has slower convergence  │
│                          │ (90 steps).                                                                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Prior program 2 (score 0.68)**: Uses 20 parameters with three separate shared        │
│                          │ parameters (ry_shared, rz_pre_shared, rz_post_shared). Better convergence (60 steps) but  │
│                          │ more parameters.                                                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **Prior program 1 (score 0.64)**: Uses 38 parameters with alternating a/b pattern.     │
│                          │ Most parameters but doesn't improve performance.                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key insight is that the current program maximizes **parameter efficiency** (0.07)     │
│                          │ which heavily weights the score, but suffers from slower convergence (90 steps). Prior    │
│                          │ program 2 shows that separating pre and post RZ gates into distinct parameters helps      │
│                          │ convergence (60 steps).                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The crossover strategy:                                                                   │
│                          │ - Keep the minimal parameter count from the current program where possible                │
│                          │ - Introduce **selective parameter differentiation** for pre and post RZ layers, which     │
│                          │ empirically improves convergence without excessive parameter bloat                        │
│                          │ - This creates a hybrid: 3 parameters (ry_shared, rz_pre_shared, rz_post_shared) instead  │
│                          │ of 2, but enables faster convergence while maintaining strong parameter efficiency        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This should achieve:                                                                      │
│                          │ - Better convergence than current (targeting 60-70 steps)                                 │
│                          │ - Maintained parameter efficiency (3 vs 14 vs 20 vs 38)                                   │
│                          │ - Preserved perfect accuracy                                                              │
│                          │ - Reduced train-test gap through better learning dynamics                                 │
