│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ sparse_per_qubit_ansatz                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ This approach replaces global parameter sharing with a strategically sparse               │
│                          │ per-qubit/per-layer parameterization. Instead of one shared RY and one shared RZ across   │
│                          │ all 8 qubits (2 params), I'll use:                                                        │
│                          │ - Two layers: Layer 1 uses different RY/RZ for qubits [0,2,4,6] and another set for       │
│                          │ [1,3,5,7] (4 params total for RY and RZ)                                                  │
│                          │ - This allows the optimizer to differentiate between even/odd qubits without exploding    │
│                          │ parameter count                                                                           │
│                          │ - The fixed CZ entanglement chain remains unchanged                                       │
│                          │ - Layer structure: RY(qubit-specific) → RZ(qubit-specific) → CZ-chain →                   │
│                          │ RZ(qubit-specific)                                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Why this should work better:                                                              │
│                          │ 1. **Faster convergence**: Enables per-qubit gradient signals instead of averaging across │
│                          │ all 8 qubits                                                                              │
│                          │ 2. **Better expressivity**: Even/odd qubit separation leverages the chain structure       │
│                          │ naturally                                                                                 │
│                          │ 3. **Parameter efficiency**: Only 4 unique parameters (not 14), better than current       │
│                          │ 4. **Same accuracy**: The problem structure is still solvable with this richer but sparse │
│                          │ parameterization                                                                          │
│                          │ 5. **Optimization landscape**: More informative gradients for the optimizer to follow     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key is that by separating even/odd qubits, we reduce the effective averaging that was │
│                          │ slowing convergence, while still maintaining extreme parameter economy.                   │
